exactly, this is what can happen....

the optimizer have problems, as the model evalutes to something weird,
already from the start, and then nothing works because it cannot start.

this works. note that the hessian in this parameterisation is ok, as
long as you do not have to many zeros...

> lpoi := (lambda, y) -> y * log(lambda) - lambda;
                 lpoi := (lambda, y) -> y log(lambda) - lambda

> diff(lpoi(lambda, y), lambda,lambda);
                                        y~
                                   - --------
                                            2
                                     lambda~

maybe you need to tweak something...

library(INLA)
r=inla.models()
ls(envir=INLA:::inla.get.inlaEnv())

m = get("inla.models", envir=INLA:::inla.get.inlaEnv())
m$likelihood$poisson$link = c(m$likelihood$poisson$link, "identity")
assign("inla.models", m, envir = INLA:::inla.get.inlaEnv())

n = 1000
x = rnorm(n, sd = 0.5)
lambda = 5 + x
y = rpois(n, lambda = lambda)
r = inla(y ~ 1 + x + offset(off),
         data = data.frame(y, x, off = 10),

         control.family = list(control.link = list(model =

"identity")))

