Hi,

"I am assuming that spatial dependence could also be in areas where there is low "activity" in terms of the absolute numbers of each species, but high spatial dependence?  That is, hot spots on the spatial field would not neccessarily correlate with areas where there is the highest number of either species. "
If I understand hotspot correctly, it is where the spatial field takes large positive values. There you find large activity. If the activity is small, in a spatially dependent way, this will be picked up by the spatial field as large negative values.

To interpret the spatial field it might be easier to think of it as a covariate, a beta*x. In some sense, the spatial field is just a fancy high-dimensional/nonparametric beta*x. You interpret the spatial field in much the same way as a shared covariate effect.
Haakon

On Tue, 12 Feb 2019 at 10:00, 'Paul Mayo' via R-inla discussion group <r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com> wrote:
>
> Hi All,
>
>
> We are using a joint model to determine the common spatial trends for two different species based on work put forward in Sadykova et al. (2017) - https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081. In the paper, they use a shared spatial field for two different species (using the copy function) in order to identify areas of high overlap between the species.
>
>
> The full code for the joint spatial model is available from the authors here: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi=10.1002%2Fece3.3081&file=ece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt
>
>
>
>
>
> The spatial field portion of the model formula and INLA call are below designating a shared spatial field (several covariates are also included) for 3 different species using the copy function (we are only using two species in our case):
>
>   f(i.spat1, model="bym", graph = "graph2.dat", hyper= param.spat01) +
>   f(i.spat2, copy="i.spat1", fixed=F) +
>   f(i.spat3, copy="i.spat1", fixed=F)
>
>   result.j1 <- inla(formula,family=c("gamma","gamma","gamma"), data=data,
>                control.compute=list(dic=T),
>                control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE), verbose=TRUE,
>                control.fixed = list(expand.factor.strategy = "inla"))
>
>
> The shared spatial fields are then plotted (Figure 5 from the paper below):
>
>
>
> We have been able to plot similar figures for the spatial field (after including covariates as well) and have found similar hot spots. However, I was just hoping for some ideas on exactly how these spatial dependencies could be interpreted.
> Sadykova et al. (2017) in section 4.2.3 of their paper describe hot spot areas as "high activity areas of the coupled species".
> I am assuming that spatial dependence could also be in areas where there is low "activity" in terms of the absolute numbers of each species, but high spatial dependence?  That is, hot spots on the spatial field would not neccessarily correlate with areas where there is the highest number of either species.
>
>
> In the case of a spatial field for a single response variable, Zuur et al. 2017 (Spatial, Temporal and Spatial-Temporal Ecological
>
> Data Analysis with R-INLA) describe an example of bird nets as follows:
>
>
> “A value of µi = 0.8 at a nest in one of these hotspots has an expected number of fledged chicks that are around exp(0.8) = 2 larger than at a nest not in such a hotspot.” – page 318
>
>
> Is there a way to characterize a shared spatial field in a similar manner?
>
>
>
> Many thanks for any help!
>
>
