Hi Finn,

I'm simply using INLA's built in:

summary.linear.predictor

Here's the rough flow of what I'm doing:

df <-read.csv('data.csv',as.is=T)

#df has 10000 rows
df['test_idx']<-0
df[9901:10000,'test_idx']<-1

# Copy ground truth for testing model fi
# Set holdout test y values to NA
df['ground_truth']<-df$y
df[df$test_idx==1,y]<-NA

# Fit the model in INLA
m <-inla(y ~1+f(group,model="iid",hyper =list(theta=list(prior="pc.prec", param=c(1,0.01))),
data=df,family="gamma",
control.compute=list(waic=TRUE, dic=TRUE, config = TRUE),
control.inla=list(strategy="adaptive",  int.strategy="auto"),
control.predictor=list(compute=TRUE)
)

# Now we can extract the linear predictor
# We use exp to match the gamma paramaterization used in INLA
pred <-exp(m$summary.linear.predictor.mean)

# Look at the Root Mean Squared Error
SE <-(df$gt -pred)^2

train_mse <-sqrt(mean(err[which(df$test_idx==0]))
test_mse <-sqrt(mean(err[which(df$test_idx==1]))

If I understand things correctly, train_mse should be close to train_mse.

Furthermore the linear.predictor in the test rows is the *same* from all rows belonging to the same group. (This is what I expected to see.)  But the linear predictor is *not the same* for the rows belonging to the same group taken from the training rows.

Thoughts?

Thanks!

